    {
      "id": "transports",
      "title": "The stdio and HTTP transports",
      "summary": "The stdio pumps and a real child server, and the HTTP side against a local upstream, are tested for cleaning, pass-through and failures.",
      "subject": [
        {
          "path": "macos/rust/zazzled/src/proxy/.dev/operation.json"
        },
        {
          "path": "macos/rust/zazzled/src/proxy/.dev/use-case.json",
          "entityId": "clean-another-server"
        }
      ],
      "criteria": [
        "stdio relays lines both ways, cleans recorded answers and returns the child's exit code; a missing command is reported.",
        "HTTP cleans JSON and event-stream answers, passes other answers and statuses through, and answers bad requests and other origins with the right status.",
        "One HTTP connection carries several requests: sized JSON answers, chunked event streams, bodiless 204s and refusals, until the client asks to close; HTTP/1.0 closes unless it asks for keep-alive, and a client that hangs up ends the connection quietly.",
        "Bad --upstream and --listen values are refused."
      ],
